Restore the two-assertion shape of termination checks on integer measures (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/414). The corrected decreases order (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/264) emitted a strict termination check as one assertion that conjoins the lower bound with the decrease. For a strict check at a call or at the end of a loop iteration whose components are all int-based, Dafny again emits the shape it emitted before the corrected order: first a bounded-below assertion for each component, then the lexicographic decrease. The two shapes state the same obligation (docs/dev/decreases-order.md, "Integer tuples"), so what is provable does not change. The solver's cost does change. Measured over several random seeds, a downstream lemma ran out of resources more often under the single assertion than under the two. In the test suite the effect goes both ways: two proofs that finished under the single assertion run out of resources under the two. A negative integer measure is again reported as "decreases expression ... must be bounded below by 0". Under `--isolate-assertions` each such check is again two tasks. Real-valued components, `by O` components, nonincrease checks, induction hypotheses and loop summaries keep the corrected encoding.
